;; *** THIS KERNEL IS CORRECT (MMA_CORRECT on an H100) AND SLOWER THAN CHAPTER 3. ***
;;
;; Measured 2026-08-18 on an H100 80GB HBM3, tf32, iters=30, vs chapter 3 as the control:
;;
;;     N       chap3     clu2 no-mc   clu2 +mc    clu4 no-mc   clu4 +mc (this kernel)
;;     2048    222.3 TF  218.5 0.98x  206-211     132.8 0.60x  122.3 0.55x
;;     4096    265.9 TF  263.9 0.99x  250  0.94x  218.7 0.82x  203.1 0.76x
;;
;; READ THE CONTROLS, not just the last column.  A full factorial of cluster size x multicast
;; separates two costs that would otherwise be confounded:
;;
;;   * Forming a cluster of TWO is free            (0.98-0.99x with no multicast at all)
;;   * Forming a cluster of FOUR is expensive      (0.60x at 2048, 0.82x at 4096)
;;   * Multicast itself costs a consistent ~5%     (0.98 -> 0.94, 0.60 -> 0.55, 0.82 -> 0.76)
;;   * Multicast never pays that back at any size or shape tried
;;
;; SO THE LEVER WAS MIS-CHOSEN, and now we know by measurement rather than argument.  Multicast
;; only reduces L2->SMEM traffic on the operand fetch path.  That it buys nothing here says
;; chapter 3's ~34% gap to cuBLAS is NOT on that path -- the kernel is bound by something else
;; (occupancy / scheduling / the wgmma pipeline), so removing bandwidth it was not waiting on
;; cannot help, and the leader-guard plus per-workgroup expect_tx cost ~5% for nothing.
;;
;; WHY A CLUSTER OF FOUR IS SO COSTLY HERE: a cluster's CTAs must be co-resident on one GPC.
;; This kernel is m64n256 with ~16K registers per warpgroup, so demanding four such CTAs share
;; a GPC constrains placement badly.  That cost is unrelated to multicast and is paid even with
;; `:multicast` removed entirely (the clu4 no-mc column).
;;
;; KEEP THIS CHAPTER.  It is a correct, working demonstration of clusters + DSMEM multicast, and
;; the honest negative result is worth more than a quietly deleted experiment: it tells the next
;; person which bottleneck to attack instead.
;;
;; Chapter 4 — CLUSTERS + TMA MULTICAST (DSMEM).  Accreted directly onto chapter 3's warp-
;; specialized wgmma kernel; the ONLY changes are the three marked below.  Everything that made
;; chapter 3 fast is untouched, so the benchmark difference is attributable to multicast alone.
;;
;; WHY THIS IS THE NEXT RUNG.  Chapter 3 sits at ~66% of cuBLAS and, importantly, FLAT across
;; sizes -- a steady-state loss, not an overhead.  Every workgroup fetches its own A tile and its
;; own B tile from L2, even though neighbours in the same row want byte-identical A and
;; neighbours in the same column want byte-identical B.  A cluster lets ONE workgroup fetch and
;; the hardware deliver to the whole group, which is bandwidth cuBLAS has been spending and we
;; have not.
;;
;; THE THREE CHANGES:
;;   1. (cluster-size :set-to (2 2))    -- 4 workgroups, 2 rows x 2 columns
;;   2. :multicast true on BOTH loads   -- an assertion; the compiler refuses if it cannot honour it
;;   3. `empty` becomes :mode :cluster  -- see below, and it is the subtle one
;;
;; WHY `empty` MUST REACH ACROSS WORKGROUPS.  With multicast only the group leader issues the
;; copy, but it writes into every group member's shared memory.  It may not reuse a ring slot
;; until EVERY member has finished reading it -- not merely its own consumers.  A workgroup-local
;; `empty` would let the leader overwrite a slot a peer was still consuming.  `full` stays
;; :mode :block because a multicast completes its transaction on each destination's OWN mbarrier.
;;
;; ARRIVALS ARE STILL WRITTEN PER-WORKGROUP.  `:arrivals 4` remains "4 consumer warps per
;; workgroup per stage"; the compiler multiplies by the cluster size.  Cluster-wide arrivals
;; slightly over-synchronise -- a row neighbour waits on a column neighbour that never touched
;; its slot -- which is the deliberate simpler choice.  The precise alternative is one `empty`
;; ring per operand, since A's group is a ROW and B's a COLUMN and a single ring cannot be scoped
;; to both; that is the contained follow-up to measure against this if it shows up.
;;
;; *** NOT YET RUN ON HARDWARE. ***  Compile-verified and PTX-inspected only.

(def-type a-mat (matrix float :address-space :global :align :compact :contiguous-term :row-major))
(def-type b-mat (matrix float :address-space :global :align :compact :contiguous-term :col-major))
(def-type c-mat (matrix float :address-space :global :align :compact :contiguous-term :row-major))

(def-kernel matmul (A B &out C)
  (declare #'(a-mat b-mat &out c-mat)
           (local-size :set-to 160)              ; 4 consumer warps + 1 producer warp
           (cluster-size :set-to (2 2)))         ; CHANGE 1: 4 workgroups, 2 rows x 2 cols          ; 4 consumer warps (warpgroup 0) + 1 producer warp
  (let ((A-ring (make-scratch-matrix-ring float (64 32)  :ring-count 2))   ; 2 x 64(M) x 32(K)
        (B-ring (make-scratch-matrix-ring float (256 32) :ring-count 2))   ; 2 x 128(N) x 32(K) B^T
        (D      (make-wgmma-accumulator float (64 256) 0.0))
        (K      (inner-dimension A B))
        ; CHANGE 3: peers must release the slot, not just our own consumers
        (empty  (make-async-barrier-ring :ring-count 2 :mode :cluster :arrivals 4 :initial-state :signaled))
        (full   (make-async-barrier-ring :ring-count 2 :mode :block :arrivals 2 :initial-state :waiting)))
    (tile-stride C (64 256) (grid-y grid-x)
      (with-warp-specialization (:consumer 4 :producer 1)
        (:consumer
          (set! D (make-wgmma-accumulator float (64 256) 0.0))
          (dotimes (grid-k (/ (to-ulong K) (to-ulong 32)))
            (let ((slot (mod grid-k (to-ulong 2))))
              (await (ring-get full slot))
              (wgmma-accumulate-via-tile (64 256 32) D (ring-get A-ring slot) (ring-get B-ring slot)
                                         :swizzle :128b)
              (signal (ring-get empty slot))))
          (store-tile D C (grid-y grid-x)))
        (:producer
          (dotimes (grid-k (/ (to-ulong K) (to-ulong 32)))
            (let ((slot (mod grid-k (to-ulong 2))))
              (await (ring-get empty slot))
              ; CHANGE 2: A is invariant along the COLUMN axis -> its group is a cluster ROW
              (load-tile A (ring-get A-ring slot) (grid-y grid-k) :barrier (ring-get full slot) :swizzle :128b :multicast true)
              ; B is invariant along the ROW axis -> its group is a cluster COLUMN
              (load-tile B (ring-get B-ring slot) (grid-x grid-k) :barrier (ring-get full slot) :swizzle :128b :multicast true))))))))
